<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="57211" href="schedule/2014/spring/ECON/490/57211.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2014" href="schedule/2014.xml">2014</calendarYear><term id="120141" href="schedule/2014/spring.xml">Spring 2014</term><subject id="ECON" href="schedule/2014/spring/ECON.xml">Economics</subject><course id="490" href="schedule/2014/spring/ECON/490.xml">Topics in Economics</course></parents><sectionNumber>U3B</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Topics in Game Theory</sectionTitle><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This section is restricted to undergraduate students.  Restricted to Economics students until the morning of January 13th, 2014. This course is the introduction of game theory and strategic decision making. We will cover basic concepts and topics in modern game theory, such as Nash equilibrium, dominance, voting, bargaining, auction, adverse selection, etc, which have broad applications in economics, politics, psychology, and everyday life. ECON 302 is a prerequisite for this course.</sectionText><sectionNotes>Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign.</sectionNotes><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2014-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2014-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>03:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>317</roomNumber><buildingName>David Kinley Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Wang" firstName="Y">Wang, Y</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>